      "reference": {
        "book": "Hebrews",
        "chapter": 11,
        "verse": "1",
        "full_reference": "Hebrews 11:1"
      },
      "modernVersion": "NIV",
      "modernText": "Now faith is confidence in what we hope for and assurance about what we do not see.",
      "meditation": "Imagine standing in a dark room, unable to see the path ahead of you. You hear a voice you trust say, \"Walk toward me.\" Could you do it? Even though you can't see them, even though you don't know how many steps it is, even though you're afraid you might fall? That's faith.\n\nIt's not blind optimism or denial of reality. It's acting based on what you know about the speaker, even when you can't see the full picture. That's how Hebrews defines faith here. Faith is being confident about things you cannot see.\n\nFor young women, this might mean trusting God's plan for your life even though you can't see it right now. It might mean believing there's light in a dark relationship—not because everything is fine now, but because you know God's character. It might mean taking risks in career or academics even though the outcome is uncertain, because you trust God has purpose in your life. Faith doesn't eliminate doubt; it acts despite doubt.",
      "actionStep": "Identify one commitment or dream you're struggling with—a place where faith is needed. Today, write three reasons why you can trust God's plan, even though you can't see the end result. These might be about His past faithfulness, His character, or ways you've already seen Him work in your life. Let these be your \"evidence\"—the foundation of your faith.",
      "prayer": "Thank you, Lord, that faith itself is a gift—confidence in what we hope for, assurance about what we do not see. You are worthy of our trust even when we cannot see your hand at work. Faith rests on your trustworthiness, not on our understanding. Strengthen our faith. Amen."
